<Doc
  title="Install"
  page="install/"
  description="Download Claude Overview for Windows, Linux or macOS, and what to expect the first time it runs."
  lede="Every release has installers for Windows, Linux and macOS on GitHub. After the first install, the app keeps itself up to date."
>
  <h2>Download</h2>
  <p>Open the <a href={LATEST_URL}>latest release</a> and pick the file for your system:</p>
  <div class="co-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>File</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Windows</td><td>The NSIS installer, <code>.exe</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Linux</td><td><code>.deb</code> for Debian and Ubuntu, or the <code>.AppImage</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>macOS</td><td>The universal <code>.dmg</code>, for Apple silicon and Intel</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>The first run</h2>
  <p>
    The installers are not signed at the operating system level, so Windows SmartScreen and macOS Gatekeeper warn
    the first time you open the app. The update files are signed, and the app refuses an update whose signature
    doesn't match the key built into it (see <a href={path('updates/')}>Updates and releases</a>).
  </p>
  <p>
    On first launch the main window opens, and the tray icon appears: the markpitt.dev disc, with a dot in the corner
    for the worst current state of Claude's services.
  </p>

  <h2>Linux</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      On GNOME, install the <em>AppIndicator and KStatusNotifierItem Support</em> extension, or no tray icon shows. The
      main window still opens on launch.
    </li>
    <li>Tray icons on Linux send no click events, so the pop-over opens from the tray menu or the shortcut.</li>
    <li>
      Global shortcuts need X11. Under Wayland, bind a desktop shortcut to <code>claude-overview --popover</code>
      instead.
    </li>
    <li>
      Keys are stored with the Secret Service, so GNOME Keyring or KWallet must be running before you add one in
      Settings.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Start at login</h2>
  <p>
    Turn on <em>Start at login, in the tray</em> in Settings. The login entry passes <code>--hidden</code>, so nothing
    opens until you ask for it.
  </p>

  <h2>Next</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={path('using-it/')}>Using it</a> covers the tray, the pop-over and each tab. <a href={path('settings/')}>Settings</a>
    covers the keys and estimates the panels need.
  </p>
</Doc>
